hadiths[17]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1489","It is narrated from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah bin 'Abd al-Rahman that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The right of pre-emption applies to property that has not been divided; when the boundaries have been determined, then there is no right of pre-emption."""
"1490","It is also narrated from Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with both of them) from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the same manner as the previous hadith or with the same meaning."
"1491","It is narrated from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The right of pre-emption applies to property that has not been divided; when the boundaries have been determined, there is no right of pre-emption."""
"1492","It is narrated from Abu Rafi' (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The neighbor has more right to pre-emption (shuf'ah)."""
"1493","It is narrated from Yahya al-Mazini (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is to be neither harming nor reciprocating harm."""
"1494","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""No person should prevent his brother from inserting a piece of wood into his wall."" Al-A'raj said: Then Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say, ""What is this that I find you turning away from it? By Allah! I will continue to proclaim it before you."""
"1495","It is narrated from Yahya Mazni (may Allah be pleased with him) that Dahhak bin Khalifa made a small water channel from the place of ‘Aridh to his field, and he wanted to pass it through the land of Muhammad bin Maslamah. But Muhammad bin Maslamah refused. Dahhak spoke to ‘Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) about this, so he said to Muhammad bin Maslamah (may Allah be pleased with him), “Allow him the way.” Muhammad bin Maslamah said, “No.” Then ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said, “Why do you prevent your brother from something that is beneficial for him, while it is also beneficial for you, as all your land will receive water without hardship?” Even then, Muhammad bin Maslamah refused. So ‘Umar said, “By Allah! This channel will definitely pass, even if it has to pass over you.”"
"1496","It is narrated from Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever withholds surplus water in order to prevent grass from growing, Allah Almighty will withhold His mercy from him on the Day of Resurrection."""
"1497","It is narrated from Urwah (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever revives barren (uncultivated) land, it belongs to him, and an oppressor has no right to any vein (of land)."""
"1498","It is narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Whoever revives barren (uncultivated) land, then it belongs to him."""
"1499","It is narrated from Ibn Tawoos (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever revives barren land, it belongs to him, and old lands are the property of Allah and His Messenger; then, from my side, they are for you."""
"1500","It is narrated from Urwah (may Allah have mercy on him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever cultivates barren land, it belongs to him, and the oppressor has no right in it."""
"1501","It is narrated from Alqamah bin Nazlah that Abu Sufyan bin Harb (may Allah be pleased with him) stood in the courtyard of his house, struck his foot, and said: ""The middle land, indeed, where is the land for the land?"" So Ibn Farqad Aslami thought that I had not recognized my right over his right, therefore for me is the whiteness of Marwah (i.e., the uncultivated land) and for him its blackness (i.e., the cultivated land). When this matter reached Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: ""The place other than the embankments is for no one. Indeed, reviving barren land (making it cultivable), whether in the form of cultivation, digging a well, or raising embankments, is like invalidating possession, that is, it will be treated in the same way as something prohibited from being possessed."""
"1502","Yahya bin Ja'dah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) arrived in Madinah, he assigned houses to the people. When a tribe of Banu Zuhrah, who were called Banu Abd bin Zuhrah, said that Ibn Umm Abd (i.e., Abdullah bin Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him) should be removed from our houses (i.e., he should not be given a plot of land near our houses), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Then why was I sent by Allah? Indeed, Allah does not purify a nation that does not fulfill the rights of guests."""
"1503","Hisham narrates from his father that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave a piece of land to Zubair (رضی اللہ عنہ), and Umar bin Khattab (رضی اللہ عنہ) allotted plots in the region of Aqiq and gathered the people and said: ""Where are those who are seeking a piece of land?"" And Aqiq is a place near Madinah."
"1504","It is narrated from Sa'b bin Jaththamah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no pasture except that it belongs to Allah and His Messenger."""
"1505","Zaid bin Aslam narrated from his father that Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) appointed his slave named Hani as the supervisor of the (public) pasture, and addressed him saying: “O Hani! Restrain your hands (i.e., do not oppress anyone), beware of the supplication of the oppressed, for the supplication of the oppressed is accepted. Allow those who have a small number of camels and a small number of goats to enter the pasture, and be cautious regarding the livestock of the wealthy companions like Ibn Affan (meaning Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him) and Ibn Awf (Abdur Rahman, may Allah be pleased with him) (i.e., do not give preference to them over the poor because of their wealth), for if their animals perish, they can sustain themselves from their date orchards and fields, but if the animals of the one who owns only a few goats perish, he will come to you crying for his children and say: O Commander of the Faithful! O Commander of the Faithful! So should I leave them, may your father not exist (i.e., it is your duty to care for them). Providing them with water and fodder is easier than (giving them) dirhams and dinars. By Allah! Perhaps these people think that I have wronged them, because these are their lands, they fought battles for them in the days of ignorance, and even after accepting Islam, they still possess them. And if it were not for the wealth (meaning horses) on which I mount people for jihad, I would not have made even a small portion of the Muslims’ lands into pasture.”"
